Distributed Systems 2026/27 - Practical Assignment
===================================================

Plain Java (JDK 11 or newer), no external dependencies.

Layout
------
  src/ds/assignment/tring   Exercise 1 - mutual exclusion with a token ring
  src/ds/assignment/p2p     Exercise 2 - anti-entropy in a P2P network
  src/ds/assignment/tom     Exercise 3 - totally-ordered multicast
  scripts/                  build and run scripts

Compiling
---------
  $ ./scripts/build.sh

The classes are written to build/.

Exercise 1 - Token Ring
-----------------------
Quick demo on a single machine (server + 5 peers on different ports):

  $ ./scripts/tring-local.sh

On 6 machines (m1..m5 for the peers, m6 for the server), after compiling on each:

  m6$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Server
  m2$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m3 IP-of-m6
  m3$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m4 IP-of-m6
  m4$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m5 IP-of-m6
  m5$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m1 IP-of-m6
  m1$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m2 IP-of-m6 --token

Exactly one peer is started with --token: it creates the token and puts it in
circulation. Start it last, or in any order: a peer keeps retrying until the
next one is reachable.

Peer options:
  --port N      port where this peer waits for the token (default 5000)
  --name NAME   label used in the output (default: the host name)
  --token       this peer creates the token

The server listens on port 6000 by default; pass another port as its only
argument. Hosts may be given as host:port when the defaults are not used.
